{
  "title": "Costs, break-even and depreciation",
  "kicker": "ENCE 356 · Engineering Economics · Chapters 3 and 8",
  "subtitle": "Which costs change with the decision, where revenue overtakes cost, and how an asset’s value is spread over its life.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "kinds",
      "locator": "3.1",
      "title": "Kinds of cost",
      "eyebrow": "Sorting costs",
      "body": "<p><b>Fixed</b> costs do not change with output (rent, insurance); <b>variable</b> costs do (materials, energy). <b>Direct</b> costs trace to a product, <b>indirect</b> (overhead) do not. A <em class=\"term\">sunk cost</em> is already spent and irrecoverable — it must be <i>ignored</i> in a forward-looking decision. <b>Marginal cost</b> is the cost of one more unit.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "sunk",
          "prompt": "You paid Rs 50,000 for a machine that is now worth Rs 20,000 if sold. The Rs 50,000 is…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "A sunk cost — ignore it; compare the future options.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "The value to use in the decision."
            },
            {
              "text": "A marginal cost."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "The past cannot be recovered."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Use the Rs 20,000 forgone sale."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Not new spending."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "total",
      "locator": "3.2",
      "title": "Total, average and marginal cost",
      "eyebrow": "Cost curves",
      "body": "<p>Total cost TC = FC + VC. Average cost AC = TC/Q falls while marginal cost MC is below it and rises when MC is above it — so MC cuts AC at its minimum (the efficient scale). With FC = 120,000 and variable cost Rs 30 per unit, AC at 4,000 units is 30 + 120,000/4,000 = 60.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Data",
          "why": "FC = 120,000, unit variable cost = 30, output 6,000."
        },
        {
          "toc": "Average",
          "why": "TC = 120,000 + 30 × 6000 = 300,000; AC = 300,000/6000.",
          "math": "AC=50",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "ac",
          "prompt": "Average cost keeps falling as output rises because…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Variable cost per unit falls to zero."
            },
            {
              "text": "Marginal cost is zero."
            },
            {
              "text": "Fixed cost is spread over more units.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "It is constant here."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "It is positive."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Until marginal cost exceeds average cost."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "scr1",
          "caption": "Figure 3.2.1 — cost by volume",
          "note": "Average cost falls because the fixed cost is spread over more units.",
          "script": "var t = create(\"table\", { headers: \"Units;Total cost;Average cost\", data: [[1000,150000,150],[2000,180000,90],[4000,240000,60],[6000,300000,50],[8000,360000,45]], summary: \"None\" });"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bep",
      "locator": "3.3",
      "title": "Break-even analysis",
      "eyebrow": "Where profit starts",
      "body": "<p>Revenue = price × quantity; break-even is where revenue equals total cost: Q* = FC/(p − v), the fixed cost divided by the <i>contribution margin</i> per unit. Selling price Rs 50, variable cost Rs 30, FC Rs 120,000: Q* = 120,000/20 = <b>6,000</b> units. Above it profit is (p − v)(Q − Q*).</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Margin",
          "why": "Contribution margin per unit.",
          "math": "50-30=20"
        },
        {
          "toc": "Q*",
          "why": "Break-even quantity.",
          "math": "\\tfrac{120{,}000}{20}=6000",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "bev",
          "prompt": "Reducing fixed costs makes the break-even quantity…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Smaller.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Larger."
            },
            {
              "text": "Unchanged."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Q* = FC/(p − v)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Opposite."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "FC is in the numerator."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "scr2",
          "caption": "Figure 3.3.1 — break-even table",
          "note": "Profit crosses zero at 6,000 units.",
          "script": "var t = create(\"table\", { headers: \"Units;Revenue;Total cost;Profit\", data: [[2000,100000,180000,-80000],[4000,200000,240000,-40000],[6000,300000,300000,0],[8000,400000,360000,40000],[10000,500000,420000,80000]], summary: \"None\" });"
        }
      ],
      "problems": [
        {
          "id": "p-bep",
          "prompt": "<p>FC = Rs 90,000, price Rs 45, variable cost Rs 25. Break-even units, and profit at 6,000 units?</p>",
          "answer": "Q* = 90,000/20 = <b>4,500</b>; profit = 20 × (6000 − 4500) = <b>Rs 30,000</b>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dep",
      "locator": "8.1",
      "title": "Depreciation: why and what",
      "eyebrow": "Spreading the cost",
      "body": "<p>An asset loses value with use and time. <em class=\"term\">Depreciation</em> allocates its cost over its life as an expense (it is not a cash outflow — the cash went out when it was bought). <b>Book value</b> = cost − accumulated depreciation; it never drops below <b>salvage value</b>. Depreciation matters for money because it is tax-deductible.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "bv",
          "prompt": "Book value at the end of the asset’s life equals…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Zero."
            },
            {
              "text": "Its salvage value.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Original cost."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Only if salvage is zero."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Depreciation stops there."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "That is year 0."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sl",
      "locator": "8.2",
      "title": "Straight-line method",
      "eyebrow": "Equal every year",
      "body": "<p>D = (P − S)/N each year. For P = Rs 100,000, S = Rs 10,000, N = 5: D = <b>18,000</b> per year, book value falling by the same step.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Annual",
          "why": "(P − S)/N.",
          "math": "\\tfrac{100{,}000-10{,}000}{5}=18{,}000",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "slq",
          "prompt": "Straight-line depreciation charges…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "More in early years."
            },
            {
              "text": "The same amount each year.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Nothing until sold."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That is declining balance."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "(cost − salvage)/life."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "No."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "scr3",
          "caption": "Figure 8.2.1 — straight line",
