          "id": "p-sl",
          "prompt": "<p>A Rs 240,000 machine has a 6-year life and Rs 30,000 salvage. Annual straight-line depreciation and book value after year 4?</p>",
          "answer": "D = 210,000/6 = <b>35,000</b>; BV₄ = 240,000 − 4 × 35,000 = <b>100,000</b>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ddb",
      "locator": "8.3",
      "title": "Declining-balance and MACRS",
      "eyebrow": "Front-loaded",
      "body": "<p>Double declining balance takes a fixed fraction 2/N of the <i>remaining</i> book value each year (ignoring salvage until the end, never going below it): bigger deductions early. Same asset: year 1 40,000, year 2 24,000, year 3 14,400, year 4 8,640, year 5 2,960. MACRS (used for tax in the USA) assigns standard percentage tables per asset class.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Rate",
          "why": "Rate = 2/N = 2/5 = 40 %. Year 1 on Rs 100,000."
        },
        {
          "toc": "Schedule",
          "why": "Depreciation then = 40,000; book value 60,000. Year 2 = 40 % of 60,000 = 24,000.",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "dd",
          "prompt": "Why do firms like accelerated depreciation?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Bigger early tax deductions, so tax is deferred.",
